Claude’s Plan
Phase 2 Implementation Plan: GitHub OAuth & User Analysis History
Context
Why This Change?
Currently, the AI Codebase Narrator can only analyze public GitHub repositories using a shared API token. Users cannot analyze their private repositories, and there's no way to track or revisit past analyses. Phase 2 addresses these limitations by:

GitHub OAuth Authentication - Enables users to sign in and grant access to their private repositories
User Analysis History - Persists analysis results so users can revisit past insights
This transformation turns the app from a stateless public-only tool into a full-featured platform suitable for professional use, demonstrating production-grade architecture for Big Tech recruiters.

Current State (Phase 1):

No authentication - completely anonymous
Single shared GITHUB_TOKEN for all requests (optional)
Public repositories only
No data persistence (stateless API)
Analysis results lost on page refresh
Implementation Strategy:
Based on user preferences, we'll implement Phase 2 in two sequential stages:

Phase 2.1: GitHub OAuth + Private Repository Access (2-3 weeks)
Phase 2.2: User Analysis History (1-2 weeks)
This progressive approach allows testing and validation at each stage while maintaining backward compatibility with anonymous public repo analysis.

Technology Stack
Database: Prisma + Supabase PostgreSQL
Prisma: Type-safe ORM with excellent TypeScript integration and automatic migration generation
Supabase: Managed PostgreSQL with generous free tier, built-in monitoring, and vector support for Phase 3
Provides production-grade schema management and type safety
Authentication: NextAuth.js v5 (Auth.js)
Official Next.js recommendation for App Router
Built-in GitHub OAuth provider with session management
Supports both JWT and database sessions (we'll use database sessions)
Excellent TypeScript support and active maintenance
Token Security: AES-256-GCM Encryption
Node.js built-in crypto module
No external dependencies
Industry-standard encryption for storing GitHub tokens
Includes authentication tags to prevent tampering
Phase 2.1: GitHub OAuth + Private Repository Access
Overview
Enable users to sign in with GitHub and analyze their private repositories using per-user OAuth tokens.

Database Schema
Prisma Schema (prisma/schema.prisma):


generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client-js"
}

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
  url      = env("DATABASE_URL")
}

// NextAuth.js required tables
model User {
  id            String    @id @default(cuid())
  name          String?
  email         String?   @unique
  emailVerified DateTime?
  image         String?

  // GitHub-specific fields
  githubId      String?   @unique
  githubLogin   String?

  // Encrypted GitHub access token (AES-256-GCM)
  // Format: "iv:encrypted_token:auth_tag"
  githubTokenEncrypted String?

  accounts      Account[]
  sessions      Session[]

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  @@index([email])
  @@index([githubId])
}

model Account {
  id                String  @id @default(cuid())
  userId            String
  type              String
  provider          String
  providerAccountId String
  refresh_token     String? @db.Text
  access_token      String? @db.Text
  expires_at        Int?
  token_type        String?
  scope             String?
  id_token          String? @db.Text
  session_state     String?

  user User @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([provider, providerAccountId])
  @@index([userId])
}

model Session {
  id           String   @id @default(cuid())
  sessionToken String   @unique
  userId       String
  expires      DateTime
  user         User     @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@index([userId])
}

model VerificationToken {
  identifier String
  token      String   @unique
  expires    DateTime

  @@unique([identifier, token])
}
Key Features
Database Sessions: More secure than JWT for OAuth token storage
Token Encryption: GitHub tokens stored encrypted in githubTokenEncrypted field
Cascade Deletes: User deletion cascades to related records (GDPR compliance)
Optimized Indexes: Fast lookups by email, githubId, and userId
