Sign out and verify /history redirects to home
Anonymous Flow Test:

Sign out completely
Analyze a public repository (e.g., facebook/react)
Verify analysis works without authentication
Verify no database save occurs (check Prisma Studio)
Verify "Sign in to analyze private repos" CTA appears
Performance Considerations
Database Indexes
userId + createdAt DESC: Optimizes history queries
email, githubId: Fast auth lookups
repoFullName: Enables future "duplicate detection" feature
Connection Pooling
Prisma handles automatically
Supabase supports up to 60 connections on free tier
Connection pooling configured in DATABASE_URL (use ?pgbouncer=true)
Pagination Strategy
Default: 20 items per page
Maximum: 100 items to prevent abuse
Offset-based pagination (simple, suitable for current scale)
Future: Cursor-based pagination for better performance at scale
Migration from Phase 1
Data Migration
No data to migrate (Phase 1 has no persistence)
All users start fresh with empty history
Code Changes
Backward compatible: Public repo analysis still works anonymously
No breaking changes to AnalysisResult type
GitHub API wrapper extended (not replaced) with optional parameters
Existing analyze page UI remains functional
User Experience
Existing workflows unchanged for anonymous users
New features are opt-in (must sign in to use)
Clear CTAs guide users to authentication benefits
Smooth OAuth flow with minimal friction
Security Checklist
 GitHub tokens encrypted with AES-256-GCM
 Encryption key stored in environment (never in code)
 Database sessions with 30-day expiration
 CSRF protection enabled (NextAuth.js default)
 OAuth scopes minimal (read:user user:email repo)
 No token exposure in API responses
 Protected API routes check session (auth())
 Cascade deletes for GDPR compliance
 Supabase connection string in environment only
 Input validation with Zod schemas
